schema: wingman-spec

# Project context. Injected into artifact instructions and read by the explore workflow.
context: |
  Workflow: the `wingman-spec` schema (see the "wingman-spec workflow" block in AGENTS.md): a senior analyst plans
  (app-map, proposal, specs, design, ADRs, tasks); developers or one subagent per task implement test-first.

  EXPLORE: when requirements or decisions are unclear, work through the `grilling` skill until every branch is resolved;
  never guess product or architecture decisions.

  SYNC: a sync is complete when the delta specs are merged and the change's ADRs are promoted to the root `adr/`
  directory. `/spec-sync <change>` does both. `/spec-archive <change>` runs it first, so run it on its own only to inspect
  or fix a sync problem. After a plain `/opsx:sync`, run `/spec-sync <change> --adrs-only`.

  Project facts (edit me): tech stack, commands, conventions. `verify-task.cjs` runs the test, typecheck and lint commands
  below; `none` disables one. It refuses to start while the test command is still <fill in>.
  - Test command: <fill in>
  # - Test run: finish        # optional: the project tests run once, at `finish`; typecheck and lint keep the Check level. A task's `Done when:` still runs at its check.
  # - Check level: targeted    # optional: when the project commands run. `finish` always runs them; a task's `Done when:` always runs at its check.
  #     targeted (default): each check runs the task's targeted tests (changed, tagged with its scenarios, named after its files).
  #     Typecheck and lint run at the check that ends a task group, the full suite at a review task and at `finish`.
  #     group: tests, typecheck and lint at the check that ends a group. task: all three at every check. finish: only at `finish`.
  # - Targeted test command: `<runner> {files}`   # optional: how to run some test files ({files}: the paths, {dirs}: their folders). Derived from the test command for common runners; `none` runs no tests at the check.
  - Typecheck command: <fill in>
  - Lint command: <fill in>
  - Language/framework: <fill in>

# Traceability (traceability.cjs).
#   on: every requirement has a `Why: <who wants it, and why>` line under its SHALL sentence, a code anchor
#       (`@spec <capability>/<requirement-slug>` in a comment) and a test anchor. finish and archive fail otherwise.
#       Rewriting an existing Why or removing a requirement needs `Owner approval: <name>, <date>` in the proposal.
#   warn: the same findings, as warnings.   off: no Why check, no anchors.
# The `@spec` markers in the code are the anchor index; nothing else is stored. `drift` and `/spec-garden` keep specs and code aligned.
traceability: on
# Where `@spec` markers go is described in the spec-implementer agent. placement.cjs checks it (a line-based heuristic).
# warn (default): list findings | error: lint, check and finish fail on them | off.
anchor_placement: warn
# Is an anchor still about its code? anchorguard.cjs diffs the change at `check` and `finish` and reports:
#   - an anchored function that changed while the change never mentions its requirement;
#   - a MODIFIED requirement whose anchored code did not change;
#   - a removed or moved anchored unit; a requirement spread over many files; an anchor in a file the task did not list.
# warn (default) | error (fails finish) | off.
anchor_guard: warn
# Does each anchor sit on code that implements its requirement? anchorfit.cjs (a heuristic) checks it.
# An anchor belongs on the function that decides the behaviour. It does not belong on imports, constants, types, string
# tables, getters or logging, or in another capability's files.
# warn (default): list them; `verify-task.cjs anchors prune` removes the removable ones | error | off.
# A count alone is no finding. Above anchor_max (default 6) the count is reported once, and only when some anchors are weak.
# Above 12, one info line asks whether to split the requirement. For a cross-cutting requirement, write `Anchors: many` in
# the requirement, or list it in anchor_many below.
anchor_fit: warn
anchor_max: 6
# anchor_many: [cap/slug]
# `finish` reads the test output: the tagged tests of the change's requirements must show up as passing. Failing or skipped
# tagged tests always fail. warn (default) | strict: also fail when the test runner prints no test names | off.
test_evidence: warn
# `drift`: a unit changed in a commit later than its requirement's text (git history only): info (default, advisory) | warn | error | off.
anchor_stale: info

# Visual check. It runs for tasks with a `Visual:` line and for tasks that change UI files. It starts the app with
# `command`, waits for `url` and opens the visual scenarios (```visual blocks in the specs) at each viewport in Playwright
# Chromium. It takes no screenshots by default. It fails on:
#   - console errors, failed requests, axe violations, broken images and sideways scrolling;
#   - key elements that are missing, hidden, off-screen or overlapping;
#   - ARIA changes to declared scenarios outside the task's scope, compared with a capture taken just before the task.
# Nothing is stored in the repository. `visual: off` for a project without UI.
visual:
  command: <fill in>          # starts the app in the foreground, e.g. npm run build && npm run preview -- --port 4173 --strictPort
  url: <fill in>              # where it answers, e.g. http://127.0.0.1:4173/ (port is taken from it and exported as $PORT)
  ready_timeout: 120          # seconds to wait for url
  review: off                 # off (default): DOM, ARIA, geometry and expect: checks only; no screenshots are reviewed.
                              # subagent: after those pass, an INDEPENDENT spec-visual-reviewer subagent compares before/after clips.
                              # Set it only where such a subagent can be dispatched (Claude Code / Cursor: Task tool; Codex: spawn an agent).
                              # The implementer never reviews its own screenshots.
  screenshots: affected       # affected: before/after clips of the task's own areas (used only with review: subagent) | off
  require_playwright: false   # true: a missing Playwright/Chromium FAILS the check instead of skipping it with a warning
  ignore_https_errors: true   # default: accept self-signed/invalid certificates (https dev servers) in the readiness probe and the browser; false = strict
  # Skip defects outside what you build, such as a third-party widget or page chrome, for every scenario. The report lists
  # each skip as "ignored (n): code - scenario/source". Allowed: a11y:*, runtime:*, content:* and layout:horizontal-overflow.
  # A missing key, expect:, absent or role failure cannot be skipped. A scenario can add `ignore:` / `ignore-http:` lines.
  # ignore: [a11y:color-contrast, runtime:console-error]   # codes; a code ending in ":" is a prefix (a11y: = every axe rule)
  # ignore_http:                                           # skip failed requests, HTTP errors, console errors and broken images whose message contains
  #   - /static/images/                                    #   the text (at least 3 characters); a list, so a value may contain a comma
  # Limits, in seconds: page_timeout (a load or a step; default 5) and request_timeout (default 3; 0 = none).
  # A request that has not answered within request_timeout is aborted and reported as a failed request.
  # baseline: on = a defect that the page already had before the task is listed once and does not fail the task. New ones still fail.
  # Off by default: it lets old defects pass. Turn it on per project.
  # page_timeout: 5
  # request_timeout: 3
  # baseline: off
  # scope: task           # task (default): a task's check covers its own scenarios; finish covers all. all: every check also covers the change's earlier scenarios and the other specs.
  # changed_only: on      # on (default): a visual run that passed is not repeated while the app files, scenarios, limits and pre-task capture are unchanged. off: always run.
  # browsers_path: <dir>      # only if Chromium is not in PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH / the default cache and doctor cannot find it
  # Gated features: scenarios with `role: admin` run logged in as that role. Credentials are never written here: they come from
  # the env vars (default WINGMAN_ROLE_<NAME>_USER / _PASSWORD) or the gitignored openspec/.local/roles.yaml. See README "Visual roles".
  # roles:
  #   admin:
  #     login: /login                    # form login: the login page path
  #     success: /dashboard              # optional: path or selector that proves it worked
  #   editor:
  #     storage_state: openspec/.local/auth/editor.json   # SSO/MFA: a Playwright session you recorded
  #   sso-user:
  #     login_command: node scripts/sso-login.js    # writes the session to $WINGMAN_ROLE_STATE_FILE
  #   api-client:
  #     headers_env: Authorization=API_TOKEN        # header name = env var holding the value

rules:
  app-map:
    - 'Size S: only two lines, "This change ..." and "Fits ...", no headings.'
  proposal:
    - 'Line 1 is "Size: S|M|L", line 2 is "Risk: none" or the kinds that apply (security, money, data-migration, concurrency, public-api). The size table in the proposal instruction defines S (1 capability, at most 3 requirements), M (up to 2 capabilities) and L. verify-task.cjs size checks the choice against the specs.'
    - List ADR candidates for every architecturally significant decision, or write "none".
    - 'An "## Edge cases" section with one bullet per edge case the request or answers name, ending with "-> <capability>/<scenario-slug>". A UI case points at a visual scenario whose state shows that content. Size never removes a scenario.'
  specs:
    - Every scenario must use concrete inputs and exact expected outputs so it can become a failing test.
    - 'Under each SHALL sentence, one line "Why: <role> wants <outcome>, so that <reason>." with at least 5 words. Rewriting an existing Why or removing a requirement needs the owner''s approval first, recorded as "Owner approval: <name>, <date>" in the proposal.'
    - Cover happy path plus at least one error/boundary case per requirement.
    - Every user-visible UI behavior gets a visual scenario (a ```visual block with route, key elements, states and viewports).
    - 'Every requirement that computes, transforms, sorts or stores data gets an "Invariant: ..." scenario.'
    - 'With a design input, visual scenarios name the design image (reference), use the design-map.md selectors as key lines and turn its Expect column into expect lines, at least one per map row. Every text element gets font-size, font-weight and line-height.'
  design:
    - 'Size S: only Implementation guide and Pitfalls (plus a short Testing approach). Size M and L: every section, written for a junior developer.'
  adrs:
    - Size S writes only adrs/none.md; an ADR makes the change at least Size M.
  tasks:
    - 'No Size line in tasks.md. The Size line of proposal.md caps the implementation tasks (S/M/L: 3/5/8). Merge consecutive tasks on the same file.'
    - 'Each task needs Spec, Test first, Files and Done when lines. End with one code review of the whole change. Group reviews only after group 1 of an L change and after groups marked (risky). No close-out tasks: verify-task.cjs finish runs them.'
    - A review task may fix findings in any file named in this change's Files lines; it needs no Files line of its own.
    - Test titles carry the scenario id they prove, e.g. [capability/scenario-slug]; tests never read source files as text.
    - A task that builds or changes UI names its visual scenarios in a `Visual:` line; every visual scenario is named by a task.
    - With a design-map.md, Files lines follow the map (its components and token files); build with project tokens and components, never new hex/px or arbitrary classes unless the map lists them under Gaps.

operations:
  apply:
    guidance:
      - Follow the TDD contract in the schema's apply instruction; use the `test-driven-development`, `ponytail`, `systematic-debugging`, `requesting-code-review`, `ponytail-review` and `verification-before-completion` skills.
      - One task per subagent, checked by openspec/scripts/verify-task.cjs; write BLOCKED.md and stop instead of improvising.
  archive:
    guidance:
      - Archive only with `/spec-archive <change>`. It ALWAYS runs `/spec-sync <change>` first (specs merge, ADR promotion, anchors and drift check), then `node openspec/scripts/verify-task.cjs archive <change>`, which refuses an unsynced change and then runs `openspec archive --skip-specs`. Never run a plain `openspec archive` or `/opsx:archive`.
